1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:02,990
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:04,320
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:09,040
My name is Catherine and today we have another episode in our series about small talk.

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:12,380
So small talk Marco is what exactly?

5
00:00:12,380 --> 00:00:19,370
Quick little conversation that you have maybe with a colleague or friend or even with a stranger maybe in the elevator.

6
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:21,920
Just about the weather or some small little topic.

7
00:00:22,560 --> 00:00:24,640
So you're not talking about the meaning of life.

8
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:26,960
You're talking about the weather or food.

9
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:32,280
So let's check out today's dialogue and we'll be back in a minute to talk more about what's going on.

10
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:43,260
Oh dear miss, you are soaked.

11
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:45,820
Wow, it's really raining heavily outside.

12
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:47,680
Yes, it sure is.

13
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:49,700
I had to run here from work.

14
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:52,100
I need to rush as I'm on my lunch break.

15
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:55,060
Well please, why don't you go ahead of me in line.

16
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:56,240
I'm in no hurry.

17
00:00:56,540 --> 00:00:57,940
Oh, that's so nice of you.

18
00:00:58,200 --> 00:00:59,200
Thank you very much.

19
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:00,240
My pleasure miss.

20
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,840
Actually, could you recommend what to eat here?

21
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:04,220
I've never been here before.

22
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:05,960
Sure.

23
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:09,720
Well, the avocado sandwich is delicious and it's the healthiest thing on the menu.

24
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,400
Personally, I think the beef salad is the tastiest choice.

25
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:14,560
I usually get that.

26
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:18,200
Also, the milkshakes are the best milkshakes in town.

27
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:20,000
Well, thanks for the suggestions.

28
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:26,560
No, don't mention it.

29
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:27,560
Alright we're back.

30
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:34,960
So, as we saw in this dialogue, it's a small talk conversation with two strangers who are actually buying some food.

31
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:41,610
So this is something that could happen if you are waiting in line at a supermarket, if you're at the bank or even at a shop like in this case.

32
00:01:42,150 --> 00:01:42,370
Exactly.

33
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:44,990
This kind of conversation happens all the time.

34
00:01:45,130 --> 00:01:48,030
So let's take a look at some words and phrases in today's language takeaway.

35
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,560
Language takeaway.

36
00:01:54,560 --> 00:02:03,280
Alright so the first word that we have for you today is the gentleman, the older person, noticed how wet the girl was.

37
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:05,240
She said you are soaked.

38
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:10,720
Alright so it's raining outside and he says oh dear miss, you are soaked.

39
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:11,960
Alright this is an adjective.

40
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:14,180
It describes what she looks like.

41
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:16,980
So if you are soaked, it means that you are a little bit wet?

42
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:18,090
Very wet.

43
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:19,800
Very wet.

44
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:26,250
You know you can say that someone is a little wet but if they're soaked, it means their shoes, their pants, their shirt, their hat, everything is very wet.

45
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:27,470
Okay, you are soaked.

46
00:02:28,130 --> 00:02:34,750
And so it's an adjective even though it has an ed ending, it may look like a verb or in the past but it's not.

47
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:36,610
It's an adjective with an ed ending.

48
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:37,150
That's right.

49
00:02:37,310 --> 00:02:45,290
So it's raining outside, this woman is very wet, she's soaked, she's wet and she says I have to rush, I have to hurry because of what?

50
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:47,800
Because I'm on my lunch break.

51
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:50,280
She's on her lunch break.

52
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:53,480
Okay so during work, we might have something called a break.

53
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:55,590
This is a short time when we don't have to work.

54
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:58,640
Five minutes, ten minutes.

55
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:02,050
And the break that we have at lunch when we get to eat is a little bit longer.

56
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:03,270
So we call this a lunch break.

57
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:03,950
That's right.

58
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:09,600
So usually people have their lunch break let's say at noon or one o'clock and it's usually an hour like you say.

59
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:10,400
That's right, an hour.

60
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:20,160
Okay so she's on her lunch break and that's why she's in a hurry and so the other guy let her go in front of him and he asked her for some suggestions.

61
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:21,360
What is good here?

62
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:23,480
What do you recommend to eat?

63
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:24,340
That's right.

64
00:03:24,460 --> 00:03:30,540
So he's not familiar with the food but the woman is and she says well, the avocado sandwich is delicious.

65
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:34,200
Now let's talk about this fruit because it is a fruit, right Marco?

66
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:35,720
It's a fruit, an avocado.

67
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:40,980
You see this, it looks like a big green pear more or less.

68
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:41,700
That's right.

69
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:44,100
Well it's green on the inside and it's dark on the outside.

70
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:46,780
So to eat it, you have to take off the skin.

71
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:47,780
Yeah, that's right.

72
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:52,560
And inside it's kind of buttery almost.

73
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:54,730
It's not hard like a banana or an apple.

74
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:56,410
It's kind of more very, very soft.

75
00:03:56,680 --> 00:03:58,200
You can take it out with a spoon.

76
00:03:58,200 --> 00:03:59,200
That's right.

77
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:08,310
It's very, very delicious and it's very common in a lot of Latin cooking where you have for example guacamole in Mexican cooking which is made primarily of avocados.

78
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:08,930
That's right.

79
00:04:09,860 --> 00:04:19,990
So she recommends the avocado sandwich and then she goes on to describe some of the food and now here, we're going to take a look at some adjectives but in a superlative form.

80
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:23,730
For example, she said it's the healthiest thing on the menu, this sandwich.

81
00:04:24,170 --> 00:04:27,370
Okay, so this is a really important point Marco and I'm glad you brought it up.

82
00:04:27,730 --> 00:04:29,450
I can say that this food is healthy.

83
00:04:29,750 --> 00:04:31,010
That means it's good for my body.

84
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:34,570
But if I say that it's the healthiest, what does that mean?

85
00:04:34,810 --> 00:04:35,730
How is that different from healthy?

86
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:42,200
Usually you're saying that it's on the list of things that are at the restaurant maybe that are healthy.

87
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:43,110
This is the most healthy.

88
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:44,890
But you don't say most healthy.

89
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:46,240
You say healthiest.

90
00:04:46,630 --> 00:04:47,610
Healthiest, okay.

91
00:04:47,910 --> 00:04:51,170
So is chocolate cake the healthiest thing on the menu?

92
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:52,290
No, probably not.

93
00:04:52,550 --> 00:04:53,570
It's probably the least healthy.

94
00:04:53,970 --> 00:04:57,090
So on top we have the healthiest thing on the menu.

95
00:04:57,590 --> 00:04:59,270
We also have another superlative.

96
00:04:59,270 --> 00:05:01,030
We heard the word tastiest.

97
00:05:01,450 --> 00:05:01,630
Right.

98
00:05:01,830 --> 00:05:03,190
This comes from the adjective tasty.

99
00:05:03,290 --> 00:05:04,490
Something is delicious.

100
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:05,920
It's tasty.

101
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:06,920
It's good.

102
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:12,080
But here we're saying that it's the thing that tastes the best, the tastiest choice.

103
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:16,540
Okay, so I think that the avocado sandwich is the tastiest.

104
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:19,800
That means I think it's the best thing you can eat.

105
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:21,280
And that's another one actually.

106
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:26,880
Best is another superlative but it's a little bit different because it's not an is, is, is.

107
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:27,880
Right.

108
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,540
It's not like tastiest or healthiest.

109
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:31,480
It's just best.

110
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:33,080
Right.

111
00:05:33,080 --> 00:05:34,440
You're saying that it's the number one.

112
00:05:34,940 --> 00:05:38,220
So the best milkshake in town like in this dialogue.

113
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:42,720
Or you say the best soccer player in the world is so and so.

114
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:46,040
Alright, so this comes from the adjective good.

115
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:52,070
So we have good which is not bad and the best which is the most good, right?

116
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:53,480
Yeah, exactly.

117
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:54,130
But you don't say the most good.

118
00:05:54,480 --> 00:05:55,590
You say the best.

119
00:05:56,080 --> 00:05:56,510
He's the best.

120
00:05:57,080 --> 00:06:00,680
So maybe Ronaldo is one of the best soccer players in the world.

121
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:01,680
Exactly.

122
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:02,680
Alright.

