Oh, dear miss, you are soaked.

200
00:09:21,170 --> 00:09:22,830
Wow, it's really raining heavily outside.

201
00:09:23,430 --> 00:09:24,730
Yes, it sure is.

202
00:09:25,170 --> 00:09:26,730
I had to run here from work.

203
00:09:27,290 --> 00:09:29,130
I need to rush as I'm on my lunch break.

204
00:09:29,690 --> 00:09:32,090
Well, please, why don't you go ahead of me in line?

205
00:09:32,730 --> 00:09:33,290
I'm in no hurry.

206
00:09:33,590 --> 00:09:34,970
Oh, that's so nice of you.

207
00:09:35,250 --> 00:09:36,250
Thank you very much.

208
00:09:36,430 --> 00:09:37,270
My pleasure, miss.

209
00:09:37,590 --> 00:09:39,870
Actually, could you recommend what to eat here?

210
00:09:39,870 --> 00:09:41,210
I've never been here before.

211
00:09:41,570 --> 00:09:41,990
Sure.

212
00:09:42,690 --> 00:09:46,730
Well, the avocado sandwich is delicious and it's the healthiest thing on the menu.

213
00:09:47,310 --> 00:09:50,430
Personally, I think the beef salad is the tastiest choice.

214
00:09:50,750 --> 00:09:51,590
I usually get that.

215
00:09:52,170 --> 00:09:55,250
Also, the milkshakes are the best milkshakes in town.

216
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:57,360
Wow, thanks for the suggestions.

217
00:09:57,360 --> 00:10:00,120
No, don't mention it.

218
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:03,840
All right, we're back.

219
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:14,400
So, talking about the superlatives, I know it's a complicated subject because grammar-wise, it can be a little bit difficult to determine when do you say, for example, beautiful.

220
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:16,480
Do you say more beautiful or do you say beautiful-est?

221
00:10:17,140 --> 00:10:18,340
You say more beautiful.

222
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:19,480
That's right.

223
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:22,780
Well, I don't really have a hard and fast rule for everyone.

224
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:27,220
It's one of those things that you learn while you're studying English and you just have to remember.

225
00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:36,090
Yes, there are some exceptions, but usually you can guide yourself by adjectives that have usually three or more syllables.

226
00:10:36,090 --> 00:10:39,370
You probably have to use most or more.

227
00:10:39,970 --> 00:10:40,270
Amazing.

228
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:43,120
Right, you don't say amazing-est.

229
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:43,370
Most amazing.

230
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:45,430
Most amazing or more amazing.

231
00:10:45,790 --> 00:10:46,330
What about funny?

232
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:47,310
Funny.

233
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:51,920
Often you say funnier or funniest.

234
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:52,630
Or sad.

235
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:54,170
You can say saddest.

236
00:10:54,360 --> 00:11:03,360
All right, so sad, funny, these are one and two syllables, sad, funny, saddest, funniest, you can use this ending.

237
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:11,080
But with the words like beautiful or amazing, there are three syllables, they're long, you have to say most amazing, most beautiful.

238
00:11:11,290 --> 00:11:23,860
Yeah, and there are some exceptions to the rule, you may find them, but usually if you see an adjective that's a little bit long like this, you can just say, you can just use more for the comparative or most for the superlative.

